<BaseLayout
  title="Animals Don’t Plan by Enumerating the Future — AI Research"
  description="A human explanation of predictive maps, successor representations, replay, and why long-horizon planning may rely on compressed futures rather than brute-force search."
>
  <article>
    <div class="meta">Essay · Planning · Neuroscience</div>
    <h1>Animals Don’t Plan by Enumerating the Future</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One evening, I was thinking about a mouse. Not a particularly interesting
      mouse. It was just a mouse in a maze, moving through corridors built by
      someone else, making a decision that looked ordinary from the outside and
      strangely deep once I started thinking about it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The mouse reached a junction. It had to choose. And the question that came
      to me was simple: <em>what, exactly, was going on in its head?</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      Did it imagine every possible future? Did it explicitly search through the
      tree of possible paths and compare them one by one? If so, the number of
      futures would blow up very quickly. But the mouse does not look like it is
      performing a giant symbolic search. It looks like it has a map of what the
      world tends to do next.
    </p>

    <p>
      And maybe that is the first important idea. <strong>We do not always plan by
      searching the future. Sometimes we plan by remembering what the future tends
      to look like.</strong>
    </p>

    <h2>A map of things that have not happened yet</h2>
    <p>
      One of the most elegant ideas in neuroscience is the successor
      representation. At a high level, it asks: if I am in state <strong>s</strong>
      now, what states am I likely to visit later? Not one specific future, not one
      fixed trajectory, but a distribution of likely futures weighted by how far in
      the future they are.
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`M(s, s') = \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t \mathbf{1}(s_t = s') \mid s_0 = s\right]`}
      label="Successor representation: expected discounted future occupancy of state s' when starting from state s."
    />

    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Start at <strong>s</strong> and ask
      how often you expect to reach <strong>s'</strong>. Count likely visits,
      but give less weight to visits farther in the future.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, <strong>s</strong> is the current state, <strong>s'</strong> is some
      future state, <strong>γ</strong> is the discount factor, and
      the indicator function is a yes-or-no test: it contributes 1 when the system
      is in state <strong>s'</strong> at time <strong>t</strong>, and 0 otherwise.
      The expectation is taken over possible trajectories starting from <strong>s</strong>.
      So the whole expression says: starting from state
      <strong>s</strong>, how much expected future occupancy does the system assign
      to state <strong>s'</strong> over time?
    </p>

    <p>
      This is a compressive representation of the future. It does not tell you one
      exact path. It tells you the structure of the world: what states tend to
      follow from a given starting point, and how soon they tend to appear.
      That is a very different thing from enumerating every possible branch.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is an important limitation here, though. The successor
      representation is predictive, but it is not a complete model of the world.
      It tells us what states tend to follow under a particular policy. A world
      model asks a different question: <em>what would happen if I took this action
      instead?</em>
    </p>

    <p class="stacked-example">
      <strong>SR:</strong> what tends to happen?<br />
      <strong>World model:</strong> what would happen if...?
    </p>

    <p>
      That distinction matters because it separates two different kinds of
      computation. The successor representation compresses expected futures. A
      world model tries to generate counterfactual futures under alternative
      actions. The first is a map of likely transitions; the second is a machine
      for imagining possibilities.
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`V(s) = \sum_{s'} M(s, s') R(s')`}
      label="State value equals the successor representation weighted by the reward associated with future states."
    />

    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Add up the likely future visits,
      giving more credit to places that are more rewarding. The predicted future
      and the current goal stay separate, so a new goal can change the value
      without rebuilding the map.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, <strong>R(s')</strong> is the reward associated with visiting state
      <strong>s'</strong>. The value function <strong>V(s)</strong> tells you how
      good a starting state is, given the likely future states that follow from it.
      The breakthrough is that if the reward function changes, you do not
      necessarily need to rebuild the entire world model from scratch. You can keep
      the predictive structure and simply reweight it with the new goal.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is a beautiful computational shortcut. It separates <em>what the world
      is likely to do</em> from <em>what I care about right now</em>. The first can be
      learned once; the second can change from day to day, or even from minute to
      minute.
    </p>

    <h2>The mouse does not need to remember everything</h2>
    <p>
      Imagine a mouse that has explored a maze many times. The maze starts as a
      collection of corridors, dead ends, and confusing loops. But with enough
      experience, the maze becomes something else: a relationship between places, a
      set of likely transitions, a rough topology of where things lead.
    </p>

    <p>
      The mouse does not need to remember every step it has ever taken. It needs
      enough structure to know which moves tend to be useful and which tend to be
      traps. That is where predictive maps become so interesting. The future is
      compressed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Instead of thinking in the form
    </p>

    <p class="stacked-example">
      A → B → C → D → E → goal
    </p>

    <p>
      we can think more like this:
    </p>

    <p class="stacked-example">
      from A, these are the states that tend to become possible next
    </p>

    <p>
      That is not a literal list of exact trajectories. It is a compressed
      predictive structure. It preserves the parts of the past that matter for the
      future, rather than the exact sequence of every movement.
    </p>

    <p>
      And perhaps that is a more useful kind of memory. The brain is not storing
      every past path in perfect detail. It is learning the structure that makes
      future paths likely.
    </p>

    <h2>But animals still do search</h2>
    <p>
      Of course, the mouse is not living entirely inside a predictive map.
      Sometimes it does need to imagine. When a rat sits still, hippocampal place
      cells can activate in sequences that resemble trajectories through the
      environment. The animal is not physically moving, and yet, in some sense, it
      is going somewhere.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is one reason hippocampal replay is so interesting. The brain seems to
      be able to take a compressed representation of the world and temporarily
      unfold it into a trajectory. Sometimes that replay corresponds to something
      that happened before; sometimes it resembles something that could happen next.
      The boundary between remembering and imagining becomes strangely thin.
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`s_t \xrightarrow{a_t} \hat{s}_{t+1} \xrightarrow{a_{t+1}} \hat{s}_{t+2} \rightarrow \cdots`}
      label="Replay unfolds a compressed representation into a possible trajectory: state s_t leads to imagined next states through candidate actions."
    />

    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Replay takes what has been learned
      and imagines a route one step at a time: from here, take this action, then
      see where it might lead. That puts replay between memory and planning.
    </p>

    <p>
      But replay is probably not random. It is not “play back everything equally.”
      Some memories are more useful than others. This is where the idea of
      prioritized replay becomes important: if replay is costly, then the system
      should spend that computation on the experiences that are most likely to
      improve future behavior.
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\text{priority}(m) \propto \frac{\text{expected decision benefit}}{\text{computational cost}}`}
      label="A replay priority rule: spend simulation budget where the gain in decision quality is highest relative to the cost."
    />

    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Replay memories that are likely to
      help a decision, especially when they are cheap to simulate.
    </p>

    <p>
      That raises a different question. Not simply, “what should I simulate?” but
      “is this simulation worth doing?”
    </p>

    <h2>Perhaps planning starts before the decision</h2>
    <p>
      The classical picture of planning is often something like this:
    </p>

    <p class="stacked-example">
      decision → search → action
    </p>

    <p>
      But neuroscience suggests this is too narrow. Replay and simulation may happen
      offline, long before the eventual decision. The system learns while nothing
      urgent is happening. Then, when the real choice arrives, the decision can be
      surprisingly cheap.
    </p>

    <p>
      The architecture may look more like this:
    </p>

    <p class="stacked-example">
      experience → replay / simulation → updated predictive representation → later
      cheap evaluation → action
    </p>

    <p>
      That is a major distinction. The brain can spend computation when it is cheap,
      rather than only paying the full planning cost at the moment of choice.
      Planning is not necessarily a single instantaneous act. It can be a slow,
      distributed process that gradually changes the representations available when
      the decision window finally arrives.
    </p>

    <h2>Grid cells and the geometry of memory</h2>
    <p>
      There is another piece of this puzzle I find difficult to ignore: grid cells.
      Entorhinal representations seem to provide a structured coordinate system for
      space, and computational models suggest they support more than simple
      distance estimates. They may encode relational geometry: the way one part of
      the environment relates to another, even when those states are far apart.
    </p>

    <p>
      This matters because it suggests that some planning shortcuts are not about
      explicit search at all. They are about representation. Instead of rolling out
      every intermediate state, the system can reason with a compressed geometric
      structure that already encodes the relevant relationships.
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`V^{\pi}(s) = \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t r_t \mid s_0 = s, \pi\right]`}
      label="Expected discounted return from state s after following policy pi. The system accumulates rewards over time, weighted by gamma."
    />

    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> A state's value is the reward you
      expect to collect from there onward, with rewards farther in the future
      counting less.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, <strong>V<sup>π</sup>(s)</strong> is the expected discounted return
      starting at state <strong>s</strong> under policy <strong>π</strong>.
      <strong>r<sub>t</sub></strong> is the reward received at time step
      <strong>t</strong>, and <strong>γ</strong> is the discount factor that makes
      rewards farther in the future matter less than immediate ones. This equation
      is useful because it clarifies that value estimation is not just about the next
      step. It is about a whole future stream of consequences. But the real power
      comes from the representation that makes those consequences cheap to evaluate.
    </p>

    <h2>The Tolman-Eichenbaum Machine</h2>
    <p>
      If I wanted a concrete neural-network model that captures this way of
      thinking, I would start with the Tolman-Eichenbaum Machine. The key idea is
      that the system does not just learn “this exact sequence occurred.” It learns
      that a certain kind of relationship exists between states and observations.
      That is a deeper and more general form of structure learning.
    </p>

    <p>
      This distinction matters. A model that only learns a story of transitions can
      do well on familiar paths but struggle when the environment requires
      abstraction. A model that learns the relationship itself can generalize more
      readily to new situations because it has learned the underlying structure,
      not just the specific sequence it happened to observe.
    </p>

    <p>
      In a sense, the brain is learning <em>how to reason over structure</em>, not
      only how to generate the next step in a trajectory.
    </p>

    <h2>What does this have to do with LLMs?</h2>
    <p>
      A large language model, in a simplified sense, does this:
    </p>

    <p class="stacked-example">
      x<sub>1</sub>, x<sub>2</sub>, ..., x<sub>t</sub> → next-token prediction
    </p>

    <p>
      This is extremely powerful. But it also tends to turn long-horizon reasoning
      into a serial chain of symbols. One thought leads to the next, each step
      depending on the previous one. Errors compound. The model is not obviously
      building a predictive map of the environment in the same way a hippocampal
      system might.
    </p>

    <p>
      Imagine a language model agent with a latent state <strong>z<sub>t</sub></strong>,
      a predictive representation over likely futures, a memory of previous
      trajectories, and a learned world model capable of simulating counterfactual
      actions. When a new task arrives, it would not necessarily generate a long
      chain of reasoning from scratch. It could first retrieve relevant predictive
      structure and memories, estimate whether additional simulation is worth its
      cost, and only then roll out the futures that matter.
    </p>

    <p>
      In that picture, reasoning is no longer synonymous with generating more
      tokens. Some computation has been amortized into the representation; some
      lives in memory; some is performed through selective simulation at decision
      time. The system is learning not only what to say next, but when it is worth
      thinking deeper.
    </p>

    <p>
      A more brain-like planner might instead look roughly like this:
    </p>

    <p class="stacked-example">
      world model → predictive map → relevant memory retrieval → selective
      simulation → policy / action
    </p>

    <p>
      The difference is not simply more depth. It is a different architecture for
      memory and evaluation. The system is representing the future in a more
      structured way, and then deciding which parts of that future deserve active
      simulation.
    </p>

    <h2>The deeper lesson: long-horizon intelligence may be about temporal abstraction</h2>
    <p>
      This is probably the part I keep coming back to. The real challenge is not
      just “how do we search farther?” It is “how do we plan at the right level of
      abstraction?”
    </p>

    <p>
      Consider a trip. You do not normally plan every individual movement in a
      literal time-step sequence. You plan at a higher level: get to Tokyo, then to
      the hotel, then to dinner, then to Kyoto. Each of those steps contains its
      own local decision process. This is temporal abstraction. The brain is very
      good at this.
    </p>

    <p>
      That may be the core lesson from the neuroscience literature: the stronger the
      abstraction, the less brute-force search is needed. A good predictive
      representation can let an agent reason over long horizons without replaying a
      giant number of low-level states.
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\min_{\pi} \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{cost}(\pi)] \quad \text{subject to} \quad Q(\pi) \ge Q_{\min}`}
      label="Choose a policy that minimizes expected computational cost while keeping task performance above a minimum quality threshold."
    />

    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Find a way to act that uses as
      little computation as possible, while still meeting the minimum quality
      bar.
    </p>

    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\mathrm{EVC}(\text{think}\mid s) = \mathbb{E}[V_{\text{after thinking}} - V_{\text{before thinking}}] - C_{\text{thinking}}`}
      label="Expected value of computation: the benefit of additional reasoning minus the cost of that reasoning."
    />

    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Keep thinking when the improvement
      you expect from more thought is worth more than the computation it costs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, <strong>π</strong> is the policy, <strong>cost(π)</strong> is the
      computational cost of following that policy, and <strong>Q(π)</strong> is the
      quality of the resulting behavior. The constraint says: do not just minimize
      cost; minimize cost while ensuring the system still performs above a minimum
      acceptable quality threshold. This is a much more realistic framing of
      intelligence than “more search is always better.” In the brain, the choice is
      often not “search more or less,” but “which abstraction is worth using, and
      when is simulation actually worth its compute?”
    </p>

    <p>
      The sharper version of that point is simple: a capable planner must learn not
      only what action to take, but whether additional computation is worth paying
      for. That is the bridge between predictive maps, replay, and the final
      decision itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Maybe the future is not something we search</h2>
    <p>
      I keep coming back to the mouse at the junction. There are multiple corridors
      ahead. Somewhere in its brain, memories are being retrieved. A predictive map
      is being consulted. A trajectory may be replayed. Perhaps the animal has
      already simulated this situation many times before, without knowing exactly
      when the decision would finally arrive.
    </p>

    <p>
      Then it moves. We do not know what the mouse is experiencing in any intimate
      way, but the computational picture is compelling. Maybe intelligence is not
      primarily the ability to search farther into the future. Maybe it is the
      ability to <em>compress the future into useful representations</em>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To know which memories matter. To simulate only the branches worth
      simulating. To plan at the right temporal scale. And perhaps most
      importantly: to learn when not to plan at all.
    </p>

    <p>
      That is the question worth asking for AI. Not just, “How do we make models
      think longer?” but also, “What structure should they learn so they do not
      need to think longer than necessary?” A system that can remember without
      replaying everything, predict without enumerating everything, and plan
      without expanding everything may be much closer to real intelligence than a
      system that simply generates more tokens.
    </p>

